    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d321">

                <pb facs="0412" n="310" sameAs="frus:frus1919v01/pg310"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/3049</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Reinsch</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Acting Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-03-07"><hi rendition="#italic">March 7,
                            1919</hi></date>.<lb/>[<hi rendition="#italic">Received April
                        7</hi>.]</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 2572</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: In connection with my telegram of even
                        date<anchor n="28" xml:id="d321-f1"/> I have the honor to enclose the
                    following papers:</p>
                <p>Telegram from the southern delegation at the Shanghai Peace Conference to the
                    Dean of the Diplomatic Body, dated March 3rd;<anchor n="29" xml:id="d321-f2"
                    /></p>
                <p>Memorandum from the Japanese Minister concerning disbandment of the War
                    Participation Army;</p>
                <p>Communication made by the Japanese Minister through a Secretary to the Acting
                    Minister for Foreign Affairs;<anchor n="30" xml:id="d321-f3"/></p>
                <p>An <hi rendition="#italic">Aide-Memoire</hi> presented March 7th;</p>
                <p>The substance of the Armament Loan Agreement and the War Participation Loan
                    Agreement as given out in the Japanese Press; and</p>
                <p>Further inter-change of telegrams between Mr. Tong Shao-yi, the British Minister,
                    and the Premier as given out by the Intelligence Bureau of the Constitutional
                    Government of China and reprinted in the <hi rendition="#italic">North China
                        Star</hi> of March 6th.<anchor n="31" xml:id="d321-f4"/></p>
                <p>It will be noted, it is claimed that the Japanese Arms syndicate was given
                    preferential rights to supply arms to China in the future. Such preferential
                    arrangement could, of course, not be upheld in the face of general treaty
                    provisions.</p>
                <p>An article from <hi rendition="#italic">The Peking Leader</hi> of March 6th,
                    concerning the War Participation Loan, which is a moderate expression of current
                    Chinese opinion, is also herewith enclosed.<anchor n="32" xml:id="d321-f5"/></p>
                <p>The attitude of the Shanghai Peace Conference to these matters will be reported
                    on in a separate despatch.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Paul S. Reinsch</hi></signed></closer>





                <xi:include href="d321-a1.xml" xpointer="d321-a1"/>
                <xi:include href="d321-a2.xml" xpointer="d321-a2"/>
